; server.bl — one `defserver`, and an OPEN vocabulary of clauses.
;
; ══ WHY THIS EXISTS ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; `defserver` is a COMPILER special form, and a special form is checked before
; macros (priv/boot/compiler.bl:4014), so no macro named `defserver` can shadow
; it. That wall is why `defregistry`, `defbus`, `defsupervisor` and `defbeat`
; were hand-copied `defserver` bodies instead of thin transformations: a bundle
; could not add a CLAUSE, so it copied the whole server.
;
; This namespace removes the wall from the std side. `proc.server/defserver` accepts
; every clause the boot form does, PLUS any clause name registered here, and
; lowers the whole thing to one boot `defserver`. So a bundle becomes:
;
;     (proc.tick/defserver reaper
;       (init [_] (ok {:done 0}))
;       (tick 15 :minutes [st] (do (reap!) (noreply {:done (inc (:done st))}))))
;
; ∵ a macro cannot take the name `defserver` while the special form holds it,
; consumers reach it QUALIFIED — `proc.tick/defserver`, `proc.reg/defserver`,
; `proc.server/defserver`. When boot's form is renamed to `defgen` (PLAN-126 §4.6,
; gated on PLAN-088) the qualifier drops off, mechanically, by a smell
; (FUP-094/095). Until then the qualifier is the rung, and it is honest.
;
; ══ THE CLAUSE CONTRACT ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; A transform is `(fn [clause ctx] → contribution)` where `ctx` is
; `{:name <the server's name symbol>}` and the contribution may carry any of:
;
;     {:prelude [forms…]   spliced at the TOP of init — arming, setup
;      :init    <form>     a map MERGED into the server's state (an EXPRESSION,
;                            so it may read `start-opts`)
;      :clauses [forms…]   real defserver clauses, injected BEFORE the user's
;      :meta    [forms…]}  non-callback clauses (invariant, …)
;
; and, for a clause that MONITORS a process, one more slot:
;
;     {:down [fns…]}      `(fn [state ref pid reason] → state)` each, threaded in
;                            ONE `handle-info [:DOWN …]` clause.
;     {:terminate [fns…]} `(fn [reason state] → any)` cleanup after the custom
;                            terminate callback, reverse order, always finally.
;
; `:down` exists because `handle_info` clauses match IN ORDER and the first match
; wins, so two clauses that each catch every `:DOWN` cannot both work: the second
; is unreachable. Merging them is the only shape in which two monitors compose.
;
; `start-opts` — the map the server was STARTED with — is in scope for every
; clause's `:prelude` and `:init`, whichever shape the init has. A clause is a
; slice of a server someone else starts: without the start argument there is no
; way to configure one, and a hard-coded default is the only alternative. Nil
; when it was started with none, so a clause must read it defensively.
;
; `:init` is the load-bearing one: it is what makes a clause COMPOSABLE where a
; macro is a fork. A server's own state and every clause's slice arrive in one
; `init`, because the expander rewrites exactly the `(ok …)` position — and
; refuses loudly when the body does not end there.
;
; Injected clauses go in BEFORE the server's own, so the pattern a clause
; installed (`[:bl/tick n]`, `[:register pid attrs]`) is matched before any
; catch-all the author wrote. A clause you declared must work.
;
; ══ ONE PURE MODEL, TWO BACKENDS ════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; The expansion is split in two. `clause-plan` is PURE: it takes the clause
; forms and an EXPLICIT vocabulary — `{head → transform}`, plus the heads the
; backend's OWN server form understands — as data, and returns a plan: the one
; `init`, the one merged `:DOWN` clause, the injected order, the unknown heads.
; It reads no VM. The `defserver` macro below is the NATIVE ADAPTER: it supplies
; this VM's vocabulary (`native-clause?` + the registry) and renders the plan
; into one boot `defserver`.
;
; The split exists so a SECOND backend expands the SAME `(proc/defserver …)`
; declaration without re-deriving the DSL. The JS actor compiler calls
; `clause-plan` with its own head set — it has no `BeamLisp.Server/callback` —
; and renders its own server form from `:body`. `clause-plan` is the contract:
;
;     (clause-plan {:name 'workers
;                   :clauses '((init [_] (ok {})) (registry (keys :id)))
;                   :vocabulary {:registry proc.reg/registry-clause}
;                   :native-heads #{"init"}
;                   ;; or :native-heads <predicate over a head NAME>
;                   })
;     → {:init … :injected … :down … :down-clause … :body … :unknown []}
;
; A transform is unchanged; only the LOOKUP is explicit.
;
; ══ AN UNKNOWN CLAUSE IS A DIAGNOSTIC, NOT A CRASH ══════════════════════════
;
; The boot path does not check: `BeamLisp.Server/callback` answers `nil` for an
; unknown head, `compile-defserver` calls `tuple_to_list(nil)`, and the author
; sees `1st argument: not a tuple`. Here the head is checked against the
; compiler's OWN table (via `BeamLisp.Server/callback`, so there is no second
; list to drift) plus this registry, and an unknown one names both sets.
;
; ══ SEE ALSO ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; priv/std/proc/tick.bl — the first client: the `(tick …)` clause
; priv/std/proc/reg.bl — the next: `(registry (keys …))`
; docs/scheduling/the-clock-and-the-wheel.md §4.6, §4.6.1

(ns proc.server
  ; The pure clause-plan core still reads only explicit arguments. The native
  ; adapter distinguishes development vocabulary from managed compilation.
  (:require [reload.declaration :as declaration]))

;; ── the registry ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(def registry
  "Clause head (a keyword) → transform. An atom, because `extend-clause!`
   extends it at load time: the vocabulary is DATA, so a bundle adds a clause
   without touching the compiler."
  (atom {}))

(defn register-clause!
  "Put `transform` under the clause head `k` in this VM's vocabulary, NOW.
   Returns `k`.

   The runtime half of `extend-clause!`, and the only thing that touches the
   atom. `transform` is `(fn [clause ctx] → contribution)`; see the header for
   the contract. Re-registration replaces, like a var, so a reload is
   idempotent."
  [k transform]
  (declaration/require-development!)
  (swap! registry assoc k transform)
  k)

(defmacro extend-clause!
  "Register `transform` under the clause head `k` (a literal keyword). Returns `k`.

   A MACRO rather than a function, and that is the whole point. A bundle
   registers its clause at LOAD time, and `__bl_init__/0` — the replay an AOT
   beam runs in a fresh VM — re-runs a namespace's requires, its refers, its fn
   vars and its VALUE DEFS, but never a bare top-level CALL. Written as a call,
   `(extend-clause! :registry registry-clause)` is therefore GONE from a built
   image: `bl build` writes the bundle's beam, the beam comes back with an EMPTY
   vocabulary, and every `(registry (keys …))` / `(bus …)` / `(tick …)` clause
   dies with `unknown clause` in a tree that loads fine from source (measured
   2026-09-20: after `bl build`, `(proc/registered-clauses)` answered `[]`).

   Expanding to a `def` makes the registration a value def, and a value def's
   initializer IS replayed — so every bundle's clause survives the build at
   once, with no per-bundle spelling to remember.

   The head must be a LITERAL keyword: it names the var this expansion
   defines, so it has to be known when the form is compiled."
  [k transform]
  (when-not (keyword? k)
    (throw (ex-info (str "extend-clause!: the clause head must be a literal keyword — got "
                         (pr-str k) ". It names the var this registration defines, so"
                         " it has to be known when the form is compiled.")
                    {:clause k})))
  (or (declaration/expand-clause! k transform)
      `(def ^:private ~(symbol (str "__clause__" (name k)))
         (register-clause! ~k ~transform))))

(defn registered-clauses
  "Every clause name this runtime accepts, sorted — the set an unknown clause
   is reported against."
  []
  (vec (sort (map (fn [k] (name k)) (keys @registry)))))

;; ── the compiler's own vocabulary ───────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; Read from `BeamLisp.Server/callback`, never copied: a second list would drift
;; from the one the codegen actually uses.

(defn- callback? [head]
  (some? (BeamLisp.Server/callback (name head))))

(defn- meta-clause?
  "Clauses the boot form handles but `callback/1` does not answer for —
   `invariant` is partitioned out by `compile-defserver` before the dispatch."
  [head]
  (= "invariant" (name head)))

(defn native-clause?
  "The clause heads the NATIVE boot form owns: every head
   `BeamLisp.Server/callback` answers for, plus `invariant` (which
   `compile-defserver` partitions out before that dispatch, so the callback
   table does not know it).

   A PREDICATE, and the native adapter's one vocabulary token: it reads the same
   table the codegen reads, so the two cannot drift. The pure model below takes
   this — or a backend's own head set — as `:native-heads`; it never reaches for
   the VM itself."
  [head]
  (or (callback? head) (meta-clause? head)))

(defn- native-pred
  "Normalise a plan's `:native-heads` into a predicate over a head NAME (a
   string). A caller may pass a predicate (the native adapter passes
   `native-clause?`) or a plain collection of head names — whichever it already
   has — and a missing set means 'no native heads', never a guess."
  [native-heads]
  (cond
    (nil? native-heads) (fn [_] false)
    (fn? native-heads) native-heads
    :else (let [names (set (map name native-heads))]
            (fn [h] (contains? names h)))))

;; ── assembling the contribution ─────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- contribution
  "Normalise a transform's answer into a keyed record with every slot present."
  [c]
  {:kind :contrib
   :definitions (vec (:definitions c))
   :prelude (vec (:prelude c))
   :init (:init c)
   :clauses (vec (:clauses c))
   :meta (vec (:meta c))
   :down (vec (:down c))
   :terminate (vec (:terminate c))})

(defn- down-clause
  "The ONE `handle-info [:DOWN …]` clause a server gets when any clause watches
   processes. `fns` are `(fn [state ref pid reason] → state)`.

   WHY IT IS MERGED rather than one injected clause per contributor: injected
   clauses go in BEFORE the server's own, and `handle_info` clauses are tried
   in order with the FIRST match winning. Two clauses that each catch every
   `:DOWN` therefore cannot coexist — the second is unreachable, and the loss is
   silent until whatever it watched has already leaked. So a clause DECLARES its
   handler here and the expander threads the state through all of them, in
   contribution order, in one clause."
  [fns]
  (let [body (reduce (fn [acc f]
                       (list f acc 'down-ref 'down-pid 'down-reason))
                     'down-state
                     fns)]
    (list 'handle-info
          '[:DOWN down-ref :process down-pid down-reason]
          '[down-state]
          (list 'noreply body))))

(defn- terminate-clause
  "Custom termination runs with its original parameters/body. Framework cleanup
   runs afterward in reverse acquisition order, even if custom or earlier cleanup
   raises. A contribution never shadows a user's terminate callback."
  [user fns]
  (let [reason 'terminate-reason state 'terminate-state
        cleanup (reduce (fn [tail f]
                          (list 'try (list f reason state) (list 'finally tail)))
                        nil fns)
        body (if user
               (list 'apply (cons 'fn (rest user)) [reason state])
               :ok)]
    (list 'terminate [reason state] (list 'try body (list 'finally cleanup)))))

(defn- classify
  "Decide what one clause is against an EXPLICIT vocabulary: passed through,
   contributed, or unknown. `vocabulary` is `{head-keyword → transform}` and
   `native?` is a predicate over a head NAME. Nothing here reads the VM — that
   is what lets a second backend expand the same declaration."
  [vocabulary native? clause where]
  (let [head (first clause)]
    (cond
      (not (symbol? head)) {:kind :pass :form clause}
      (native? (name head)) {:kind :pass :form clause}
      :else
        (let [t (get vocabulary (keyword (name head)))]
          (if (nil? t)
            {:kind :unknown :head (name head) :where where}
            (contribution (t clause {:name where})))))))

;; ── init: the user's slice, every clause's slice, one state ─────────────

(defn- last-form [forms] (nth forms (- (count forms) 1)))

(defn- build-init
  "Rewrite the server's `init` so it returns the user's state MERGED with every
   clause's `:init`, with the prelude spliced at the top. Synthesizes an init
   when the server has none.

   The user's body must END in `(ok …)`: that is the position the arithmetic of
   a merge happens at, and guessing at any other shape would be silent
   breakage. A missing `(ok …)` is a declaration-time error naming the clause
   that made it necessary.

   `start-opts` is in scope for every clause's `:prelude` and `:init`, whichever
   shape the init has. A clause is a slice of a server that someone else starts,
   so a clause that cannot see WHY it was started cannot be configured — and the
   name has to be one it can write down. The `let` in the user-init branch is
   what makes the name the same in both."
  [user-init prelude merged where]
  (let [prelude (vec prelude)]
    (if (nil? user-init)
      (when (or (seq prelude) (some? merged))
        `(init [start-opts] (do ~@prelude (ok ~(or merged {})))))
      (let [parts (vec (rest user-init))
            params (first parts)
            body (vec (drop 1 parts))
            tail-form (when (> (count body) 0) (last-form body))
            head-forms (vec (take (- (count body) 1) body))]
        (when (not (and (list? tail-form) (= 'ok (first tail-form))))
          (throw (ex-info (str where ": init must end with (ok state) — a clause"
                                " contributes a slice of that state, and this is"
                                " where the merge happens")
                          {:init user-init})))
        (let [state (if (= 2 (count tail-form)) (second tail-form) {})
              state' (if (nil? merged) state `(merge ~state ~merged))]
          `(init ~params
             (let [start-opts ~(first params)]
               (do ~@prelude ~@head-forms (ok ~state')))))))))

;; ── the pure plan: the contract a backend consumes ──────────────────────
;;
;; `clause-plan` is the whole expansion, as DATA, with no VM in it. A backend
;; hands it the source clause forms and an EXPLICIT vocabulary, and gets back the
;; single `init`, the merged `:down` clause, the injected clause order and the
;; reported unknowns. This VM's macro is one such backend (below); a second —
;; the JS actor compiler — passes its own head set instead of
;; `BeamLisp.Server/callback` and renders its own server form. Neither re-derives
;; the DSL.

(defn clause-plan
  "Expand `clauses` for a server named `:name` against an EXPLICIT vocabulary.

   THE STABLE CONTRACT a backend consumes — this VM's `defserver` macro below,
   and the JS actor compiler — so the DSL transformation (the state merge, the
   ONE `:DOWN` clause, clause ordering, the unknown-clause report) lives ONCE.

   Input — source DATA plus a vocabulary:
     {:name         <server-name symbol>
      :clauses      [<clause form> …]
      :vocabulary   {<head keyword> <transform>}   ; see the clause contract
      :native-heads <predicate> | #{<head name> …}}
   `:native-heads` names the heads the backend's OWN server form understands and
   passes through (the native adapter passes `native-clause?`). Every other head
   must be a `:vocabulary` transform or it is reported in `:unknown`.

   Output — a plan map; every slot is always present:
     :name          the server name
     :unknown       [{:head <name> :where <name>} …] ; empty when valid
     :contributions [<normalised contribution> …]    ; in clause order
     :prelude       [forms …]     ; spliced at the TOP of init
     :init          <form> | nil  ; user state ∧ every clause's slice
     :injected      [forms …]     ; clause forms, injected BEFORE the user's
     :down          [fns …]       ; ordered (fn [state ref pid reason] → state)
     :down-clause   <form> | nil  ; the ONE merged [:DOWN …] handle-info clause
     :meta          [forms …]     ; non-callback clauses (invariant, …)
     :pass          [forms …]     ; the user's clauses minus init
     :body          [forms …]     ; the exact clause list for the server form"
  [{:keys [clauses vocabulary native-heads] sname :name}]
  (let [native? (native-pred native-heads)
        parsed (vec (map (fn [c] (classify vocabulary native? c sname)) clauses))
        unknown (vec (filter (fn [p] (= :unknown (:kind p))) parsed))
        contribs (vec (filter (fn [p] (= :contrib (:kind p))) parsed))
        definitions (vec (mapcat :definitions contribs))
        prelude (vec (mapcat :prelude contribs))
        init-forms (vec (filter (fn [x] (some? x)) (map :init contribs)))
        merged (cond (empty? init-forms) nil
                     (= 1 (count init-forms)) (first init-forms)
                     :else (cons 'merge init-forms))
        down-fns (vec (mapcat :down contribs))
        terminate-fns (vec (mapcat :terminate contribs))
        injected (vec (mapcat :clauses contribs))
        meta (vec (mapcat :meta contribs))
        pass (vec (map :form (filter (fn [p] (= :pass (:kind p))) parsed)))
        init? (fn [c] (= 'init (first c)))
        user-init (first (filter init? pass))
        user-terminate (filter #(= 'terminate (first %)) pass)
        _ (when (and (seq terminate-fns) (> (count user-terminate) 1))
            (throw (ex-info "Contribution cleanup requires one terminate callback" {:kind :ambiguous-terminate})))
        rest-pass (vec (remove (fn [c] (or (init? c) (and (seq terminate-fns) (= 'terminate (first c))))) pass))
        built-init (build-init user-init prelude merged (str "defserver " sname))
        down (when (seq down-fns) (down-clause down-fns))
        terminate (when (seq terminate-fns) (terminate-clause (first user-terminate) terminate-fns))
        body (vec (concat (when (some? built-init) (list built-init))
                          injected
                          (when terminate (list terminate))
                          (when (some? down) (list down))
                          meta
                          rest-pass))]
    {:name sname
     :definitions definitions
     :unknown unknown
     :contributions contribs
     :prelude prelude
     :init built-init
     :injected injected
     :down down-fns
     :down-clause down
     :terminate terminate-fns
     :terminate-clause terminate
     :meta meta
     :pass rest-pass
     :body body}))

;; ── the native adapter ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; The macro supplies the NATIVE vocabulary (`native-clause?` + this registry)
;; and renders the plan into one boot `defserver`. It is the only native half:
;; everything above is a pure function of its inputs.

(defn- accepted-vocabulary
  "Every clause name the native form accepts, sorted: the boot callbacks plus
   this registry. The set an unknown clause is reported against."
  []
  (sort (concat ["init" "handle-call" "handle-cast" "handle-info"
                 "handle-continue" "terminate" "invariant"]
                (registered-clauses))))

(defn unknown-clause-error
  "The diagnostic for a clause head the native vocabulary does not know: the
   head, and the whole accepted set. Built, not thrown, so a caller decides and
   the message is testable without expanding a broken form.

   THE MESSAGE HAS TO CARRY THE RULE, not only the vocabulary. A clause is
   resolved when the form is EXPANDED; `extend-clause!` takes effect when it is
   EVALUATED. Those are the same order in a file that is interpreted (bl run)
   and DIFFERENT in one that is compiled as a unit — so the same source passes
   one way and fails the other, and an error that listed only what was accepted
   left the rule unstated (measured: BUG-082, where a test-local clause was green
   for weeks from a warm AOT cache)."
  [server unknown]
  (ex-info (str "defserver " server ": unknown clause `" (:head unknown)
                "` — accepted here: " (join " " (accepted-vocabulary))
                ". A clause is resolved when this form is COMPILED, so"
                " a name registered later in the same file is too late:"
                " either require a namespace that registers it first"
                " (the std clauses do), or use one of the above.")
           {:clause (:head unknown) :server server}))

(defn- expand-server
  "Render a plan into the boot `(defserver …)` form the native compiler
   consumes. The one place this file emits a server form."
  [p]
  (let [server `(defserver ~(:name p) ~@(:body p))]
   (if (seq (:definitions p))
    (to-list (into ['do] (conj (:definitions p) server)))
    server)))

;; The std namespace that registers each std clause — the ONE table a demand
;; load is resolved against (a clause head is not a namespace name: `registry`
;; lives in proc.reg, `perform` in proc.queue).
(def ^:private CLAUSE-DESCRIPTORS
  {"registry" ["proc.reg" "registry-clause"] "bus" ["proc.bus" "bus-clause"]
   "tick" ["proc.tick" "tick-clause"] "sched" ["proc.sched" "sched-clause"]
   "queue" ["proc.queue" "queue-clause"] "perform" ["proc.queue" "perform-clause"]
   "pipeline" ["proc.pipeline" "pipeline-clause"]
   "reconcile" ["proc.reconcile" "reconcile-clause"]
   "evolve" ["proc.evolve" "evolution-clause"]})

(def CLAUSE-PROVIDERS
  ;; Retain the development demand-loader API;one descriptor table owns names.
  (into {} (map (fn [[head descriptor]] [head (first descriptor)]) CLAUSE-DESCRIPTORS)))

(defn- compiler-vocabulary [clauses]
  (if (or (declaration/preparing?) (declaration/managed?))
    ;; Literal fixed SDK identities,not a snapshot/filter of mutable registrations.
    ;; Only requested providers are loaded,so cold compilation stays demand driven.
    (into {} (keep (fn [clause]
                    (let [head (name (first clause))
                          owned (when (declaration/preparing?)
                                  (declaration/compilation-clause (keyword head)))
                          [provider binding] (get CLAUSE-DESCRIPTORS head)]
                      (cond
                        owned [(keyword head) owned]
                        provider
                        (do
                        (BeamLisp.Loader/ensure_loaded provider)
                        (let [f (BeamLisp.Env/fetch_exact! provider binding)]
                          (when-not (fn? f)
                            (throw (ex-info "Missing fixed SDK clause transform"
                                            {:kind :missing-clause-transform :clause head})))
                          [(keyword head) f]))
                        :else nil))) clauses))
    @registry))

(defn load-clause-provider!
  "Load the std namespace that registers clause `head` (a name string), when
   there is one and it is not yet registered. → true when a load ran."
  [head]
  (let [h (str head)
        provider (get CLAUSE-PROVIDERS h)]
    (if (and (some? provider) (not (contains? @registry (keyword h))))
      (do (BeamLisp.Loader/ensure_loaded provider) true)
      false)))

(defmacro defserver
  "A `defserver` that also accepts the clauses this runtime has registered.

   Every clause the boot form takes, plus any registered clause name. An
   unknown name is reported with the whole accepted vocabulary rather than
   reaching the codegen as a `not a tuple`.

   The work is `clause-plan`'s: this form is the NATIVE ADAPTER around it,
   supplying the native vocabulary (`native-clause?`, whose table the codegen
   itself reads) and rendering the plan to one boot `defserver`."

  [name & clauses]
  (let [plan (fn [] (clause-plan {:name name
                                  :clauses (vec clauses)
                                  :vocabulary (compiler-vocabulary clauses)
                                  :native-heads native-clause?}))
        first-try (plan)
        ;; A clause's provider may be a DEFERRED require of the namespace being
        ;; compiled (`(:require [proc.reg :as reg])` is demand-loaded), so its
        ;; `extend-clause!` has not run when this macro expands — and whether
        ;; it had depended on what some OTHER file loaded first (measured: an
        ;; unrelated change to datom.query.plan flipped live.app from green to
        ;; `unknown clause registry`). So an unknown head first loads the
        ;; std provider that owns it, then the plan is made again: the answer
        ;; no longer depends on load order.
        p (if (and (seq (:unknown first-try))
                   (some (fn [u] (load-clause-provider! (:head u))) (:unknown first-try)))
            (plan)
            first-try)]
    (when (seq (:unknown p))
      (if (or (declaration/preparing?) (declaration/managed?))
        (throw (ex-info "Managed clause has no admitted compiler transform"
                        {:kind :unadmitted-clause-transform
                         :clause (:head (first (:unknown p))) :server name}))
        (throw (unknown-clause-error name (first (:unknown p))))))
    (expand-server p)))
